    "subTest": "WRITING",
    "taskType": "WRITING_LETTER",
    "profession": "DIETETICS",
    "slug": "wri-dietetics-refeeding-syndrome-risk-handover-from-eating-disorder-unit",
    "title": "Dietetics — Refeeding syndrome risk handover from eating disorder unit",
    "prompt": "Using the case notes, write a letter to the GP. Write 180–200 words. Select only the information relevant to ongoing care.",
    "difficulty": "STRETCH",
    "topicTag": "referral",
    "timeLimitSeconds": 2700,
    "active": true,
    "payload": {
      "wordMax": 200,
      "wordMin": 180,
      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMiss Hannah Croft, 22, female. Student.\nAdmitted 6 June 2026. Discharged 28 August 2026.\nHandover from the inpatient unit to the community team.\nInformal admission throughout, following a GP referral in late May.\n\nDiagnosis:\nAnorexia nervosa, restrictive subtype.\nAssessed on admission as at high risk of refeeding syndrome.\nFirst diagnosed at 17. One previous episode of community treatment, which ended when she started university.\nPast history: childhood asthma, no symptoms or treatment for over ten years. Wisdom tooth removed in 2024.\n\nAnthropometry:\nOn admission: weight 41 kg, height 1.66 m, body mass index 14.9.\nOn discharge: weight 46 kg, body mass index 16.7.\nWeighed weekly on the ward in a hospital gown after breakfast, on the same scales.\nAverage gain approximately 0.4 kg per week over the admission.\nWeight steady at 46 kg for the final fortnight while unsupervised breakfasts were introduced.\nHeight rechecked on the ward stadiometer at discharge and unchanged.\n\nBiochemistry:\nPhosphate, potassium and magnesium all fell in the first week and were corrected; monitored daily for ten days, then twice weekly.\nAll three have been within range for six weeks.\nLiver enzymes normalised. Full blood count normal at discharge.\nCalcium and vitamin D levels were checked on admission and were within range.\nBone density scan on 12 August shows reduced density at the lumbar spine; results discussed with her.\nUrea, creatinine and glucose normal throughout the admission.\n\nNutritional management:\nFeeding started at approximately 1400 kcal daily, with daily phosphate, potassium and magnesium monitoring and phosphate supplementation, increased over seven days.\nNow on a structured meal plan of 2200 kcal daily: three meals and three snacks, all supervised initially, latterly unsupervised at breakfast.\nThe snacks are mid-morning, mid-afternoon and supper, each with a milky drink.\nShe has been managing meals in the dining room with staff present for the last four weeks.\nNow chooses independently from the full hospital menu, including a pudding at lunch and supper.\nHas prepared two lunches in the therapy kitchen and ate both in full with a nurse nearby.\nTwo weekend leaves home in August went well; her parents report that all meals and snacks were completed.\n\nMedication:\nThiamine 100 mg three times daily was given for the refeeding period and stops at discharge, as agreed with the unit.\nB-complex multivitamin continuing.\nPhosphate supplement given during the refeeding period and discontinued on 20 June.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nPhysical observations:\nPulse 54, blood pressure 102/64 lying, no significant postural drop for the last three weeks.\nPulse was 42 on admission and has risen steadily with weight gain.\nTemperature normal. ECG on 24 August showed no QT prolongation.\nEarlier weekly ECGs during the admission were also normal.\nDry skin and mild hair thinning reported on admission; both are improving.\n\nSocial:\nReturning to her parents' home. Her university course is deferred until January.\nShe is already registered with your practice, which is about twenty minutes from her parents' home.\nHer mother attended the discharge meeting and has been given the meal-plan guidance.\nHer father works away during the week. Her younger brother is at secondary school.\nShe hopes to resume the second year of her history degree and keeps in touch with two friends from her course.\nEnjoys knitting and has been making a blanket for her grandmother.\nShe is looking forward to seeing the family cat again.\nEnjoys crime novels and has started a book group with her mother.\nShe asked whether her unit library books can be returned to the local branch.\n\nPlan for the community team:\nContinue the meal plan as written; no changes without dietetic agreement.\nGradual restoration towards a body mass index of 19.\nOutpatient dietetic review fortnightly and psychology weekly.\nGP to weigh fortnightly and check bloods monthly.\nReduced bone density: GP to consider calcium and vitamin D supplements and give bone health advice; weight restoration is the main treatment.\nAgreed readmission criteria are documented in the enclosed care plan.\n\nOther information:\nShe understands why the meal plan should not be changed and described her early warning signs at the discharge meeting.\nShe has the unit's contact number and knows how to reach the community team between appointments.\nHer community psychologist met her on the unit last week and the first appointment is booked.\nShe uses a phone app to follow her meal plan, chosen with the dietitian.\nShe asked whether her prescriptions can be collected from the pharmacy near her parents' home.\nA copy of this summary has been sent to the community eating disorder team and to Hannah.\n",
      "recipient": "Dr Andrew Nesbitt, General Practitioner",
      "letterType": "discharge",
      "taskInstruction": "Provide a discharge summary and ongoing nutritional plan following inpatient eating disorder treatment."
    },
    "guidanceNote": "Make the ongoing monitoring schedule clear; the GP needs to know weight and biochemistry targets to manage relapse risk."
