      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMiss Chloe Maddox, 14, female.\nSeen in the paediatric diabetes clinic with both parents, 27 August 2026.\nStarts Year 10 at secondary school next week.\n\nDiagnosis:\nType 1 diabetes, diagnosed August 2023.\nBasal-bolus regimen since diagnosis.\nPresented at age eleven with thirst and weight loss; no ketoacidosis at diagnosis.\nNo admissions with ketoacidosis or severe hypoglycaemia since diagnosis.\n\nAnthropometry:\nWeight 52 kg. Height 1.61 m. Body mass index 20.1.\nWeight steady over the past year.\nGrowth appropriate and tracking along her centile.\nPeriods started last year and are regular.\n\nBiochemistry:\nHbA1c 79 mmol/mol, having risen from 64 twelve months ago and 71 in March.\nThe download covers fourteen days; she tests four to six times a day.\nMeter download shows frequent post-meal readings above 14 mmol/L, particularly after lunch.\nReadings after the evening meal are mostly in target.\nNo hypoglycaemia below 3.0 recorded in the last month. No ketones.\nThyroid function and coeliac screen normal at the annual review.\nFasting readings before breakfast are mostly 6 to 9 mmol/L. Overnight readings stable on the download.\nLipids and blood pressure normal at the annual review. Eye screening in June showed no retinopathy.\n\nIntake history:\nSchool lunch is irregular; she often buys chips or a baguette from the canteen queue and boluses afterwards, if at all.\nLunch break is forty minutes, and the canteen queue often takes twenty of them.\nSnacks on cereal bars between lessons.\nShe is unsure of the carbohydrate content of these bars.\nEstimates carbohydrate by eye and has not weighed food since the year of diagnosis.\nShe remembers the basics from her first education session but says she has forgotten most of the portion values.\nEvening meal at home is counted more accurately.\nBreakfast is usually cereal with milk and toast, counted by her mother before school.\nDrinks water and diet drinks; no regular sugary drinks.\nEnjoys baking with her grandmother at weekends.\nUses her phone for most things and liked the idea of food photographs.\nHer mother weighs the rice and pasta and keeps a list of carbohydrate values on the fridge.\n\nMedication:\nInsulin aspart with meals, current ratio 1 unit to 12 g at all meals.\nInsulin glargine 22 units at night.\nPen needles and meter strips are ordered on repeat without problems.\n\nAdherence:\nMeter download shows six missed mealtime boluses in the past fortnight, five of them at lunchtime.\nShe says she does not want to inject in front of her friends.\nShe was open about this today and wants help to find a way that works for her.\nInjects into her abdomen and thighs; injection sites checked today and look healthy.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nSocial:\nLives with both parents and a younger brother.\nHer mother works part-time and her father works shifts.\nManages her own injections and would like to keep doing so; her parents ask to be more involved but she is resistant.\nShe would be happier for her parents to see her readings remotely than to check on her at meals.\nPlays netball twice weekly and has had no exercise-related hypoglycaemia.\nHer best friend knows about the diabetes; others in her class do not.\nGoes to drama club after school on Thursdays.\nBroke her left wrist in a trampoline fall at age nine, fully healed.\nUp to date with school immunisations, including HPV.\nThe family are planning a summer holiday in Spain next year.\nHer father asked whether clinic letters could be sent by email rather than by post.\nShe carries glucose tablets in her kit bag and knows how to treat a low.\n\nPlan:\nStructured carbohydrate counting education, three sessions, with food photographs and scales for home practice.\nEducation to cover bolus timing: aspart should be given before eating, not afterwards.\nInsulin-to-carbohydrate ratios to be reviewed by the diabetes team after education has improved counting and timing.\nReview the lunch ratio only on days with a correctly timed, counted bolus; missed or late boluses explain the post-lunch highs.\nAddress the missed lunchtime doses directly with her, exploring discreet options rather than supervision.\nSchool nurse to be contacted with her permission.\nRepeat HbA1c in three months. Offer real-time continuous glucose monitoring now (NICE NG18).\nDiscuss a hybrid closed-loop system with Chloe and her parents now (NICE TA943).\n\nOther information:\nChloe and her parents agreed to this referral and to the education sessions.\nShe would prefer appointments after school or during the holidays.\nShe is confident with phone apps and would welcome an app-based carbohydrate counter.\nThe family speak English at home; no interpreter needed.\nHer younger brother is in Year 6 and is well.\nShe has the clinic appointment card and the team's helpline number.\n",
      "recipient": "Ms Olivia Trent, Paediatric Diabetes Dietitian, Children's Diabetes Service",
