      "caseNotes": "\nGP-ATTACHED DIABETES CLINIC\n\nPatient:\nMr Daniel Okonkwo, 54, accountant.\nSeen 12 June 2026. Referral written 14 June 2026.\nAttended alone.\n\nDiagnosis:\nType 2 diabetes, diagnosed 2021.\nUnder the care of the practice diabetes nurse, seen every six months.\nHypertension. Mild obesity.\nHypertension diagnosed 2019.\nPast history: childhood asthma, outgrown in his teens; knee arthroscopy in 2015 after a football injury, no ongoing problems.\n\nCurrent status:\nHbA1c 77 mmol/mol, up from 62 mmol/mol six months ago.\nWeight 96 kg, height 1.74 m, body mass index 31.7.\nWaist 108 cm. Blood pressure 142/88.\nTwo earlier blood pressure readings this year were similar.\nTotal cholesterol 5.4 mmol/L. eGFR 78. Urine albumin-creatinine ratio normal.\nHDL cholesterol 1.0 mmol/L. Triglycerides 2.1 mmol/L.\nRetinal screening in April was clear. Foot check normal, pulses present, sensation intact.\nWeight was 92 kg at diagnosis in 2021 and has risen steadily since.\nLiver function and thyroid function normal.\n\nDiet history:\nBreakfast skipped on working days.\nLeaves home at 7 am and says he is not hungry in the morning.\nTakeaway lunch four or five days a week, usually fried chicken and rice.\nThe office canteen offers salads and grilled options, which he rarely uses.\nLarge evening meal after 9 pm.\nUsually rice or yam with stew, often with a second helping, then straight to the sofa.\nTwo to three sugary soft drinks daily.\nMostly regular cola from the office vending machine.\nBiscuits at the desk through the afternoon.\nA colleague brings in cakes most Fridays, which he usually joins in with.\nVegetables rarely, perhaps twice a week.\nTwo cups of coffee with milk and no sugar in the mornings.\nAlcohol: a glass or two of wine with dinner at weekends, about four units a week.\nEats out with clients roughly twice a month.\nAt weekends his wife cooks jollof rice, pounded yam and stews, which he enjoys and would not want to give up.\n\nMedication:\nMetformin 1 g twice daily.\nRamipril 5 mg once daily.\nAtorvastatin 20 mg at night.\nAll taken as prescribed; he has not missed doses.\nUses a weekly pill organiser and reports no side effects.\n\nAllergies:\nPenicillin — rash.\n\nSocial:\nLives with his wife. Sedentary job with long hours and a car commute.\nHis wife is a teacher and is also trying to eat more healthily.\nWorks from home one day a week.\nWalks very little; no formal exercise.\nHis phone records an average of about 3000 steps a day.\nReports significant work stress and irregular mealtimes since a promotion in January.\nSleeps about six hours a night and often works on his laptop until late.\nHis wife cooks at weekends and is willing to attend joint sessions.\nTwo teenage children; the elder is about to start university.\nHas a gym membership through work that he has never used.\nSeason ticket holder at the local football club and attends home games with his son.\n\nMotivation:\nHe was shaken by his father's below-knee amputation in March and is keen to avoid insulin.\nHe visits his father most weekends and has seen him struggle with mobility since.\nHe has asked directly whether the diabetes can be put into remission.\nHe says he would rather change his eating than add more tablets.\n\nPrevious input:\nAttended a single group education session in 2021 and did not return, saying the timing clashed with work.\nHe remembers little of it beyond advice to cut down on sugar.\nHe has never seen a dietitian individually.\nTried a carbohydrate-tracking app for a few weeks last year but found it too time-consuming.\n\nPlan:\nCarbohydrate-awareness education and a structured meal pattern, with attention to the missed breakfast and the late evening meal.\nWeight-management goals — an initial 5 per cent, which is around 5 kg.\nReinforce physical activity, starting with the commute.\nReview in three months with repeat HbA1c and weight, alongside the GP.\nGP to review his diabetes medication now in view of the rising HbA1c (NICE NG28), alongside dietary input.\nGP to check his eligibility for the NHS Path to Remission low-calorie diet programme, which answers his remission question.\n\nOther information:\nInfluenza and pneumococcal vaccines up to date. Routine dental check in May.\nPrefers early-morning or lunchtime appointments and can attend remotely on Fridays.\nHe asked whether the dietetic clinic has evening appointments and where to park.\nHe has been told about this referral and is expecting to be contacted.\nHe would like written information to share with his wife.\nSpeaks English and Igbo; no interpreter needed.\nHis wife is happy to be contacted to arrange the joint sessions.\n",
