      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMr Thomas Greer, 29, male. Graphic designer.\nSeen on the gastroenterology ward, 30 August 2026, day three of admission.\nAdmitted through the emergency department with worsening symptoms.\n\nDiagnosis:\nUlcerative colitis, diagnosed 2022, extensive disease.\nPrevious flares in 2023 and 2024 both settled with oral steroids; largely in remission over the last year until now.\nAcute severe flare — eight to ten bloody stools daily for two weeks, including at night.\nPast history: tonsillectomy aged eight. Childhood vaccinations complete.\n\nAnthropometry:\nWeight 64 kg, having been 70 kg three weeks ago.\nHeight 1.80 m. Body mass index 19.8.\nWeighed on the ward scales on admission and again today.\nThe loss is rapid and represents 8.6 per cent of body weight.\nHis usual weight had been stable at around 70 kg for several years.\nTired, but mobile independently on the ward.\n\nBiochemistry:\nC-reactive protein 78 mg/L.\nAlbumin 28 g/L.\nHaemoglobin 105 g/L, ferritin low, transferrin saturation 11 per cent.\nHaemoglobin was 138 g/L at clinic in the spring.\nPotassium 3.3 mmol/L. Magnesium low-normal.\nStool cultures negative. Clostridioides difficile toxin negative.\nRenal function and liver function normal.\nObservations stable on the ward chart since admission.\n\nIntake history:\nAppetite poor. Actively avoiding food because eating provokes urgency and pain.\nFeels full and bloated after a few mouthfuls.\nEstimated intake under 1000 kcal daily for the past ten days.\nEstimate based on a food recall taken this morning.\nHas excluded dairy, bread and all vegetables after reading online that they cause flares; this has narrowed his diet considerably without improving symptoms.\nBefore this he ate cheese, milk on cereal and wholemeal bread daily without problems.\nDrinking mainly water and cola.\nDrinks about two litres a day.\nCola is usually the full-sugar kind, two or three cans a day.\nHas managed small amounts of plain rice and clear soup on the ward without worse symptoms.\nBefore this flare he ate a varied diet, cooked most evenings and ran at weekends.\n\nMedication:\nIntravenous hydrocortisone.\nResponse to intravenous steroids assessed today, day three; the gastroenterology and surgical teams will decide on rescue therapy (biologic) or colectomy.\nIntravenous iron given yesterday.\nVenous thromboembolism prophylaxis: medical team to confirm prophylactic low-molecular-weight heparin is prescribed today.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nSocial:\nLives with a flatmate. Works from home. Has taken three weeks off.\nThe flat is on the second floor with a lift; the bathroom is next to his bedroom.\nHis flatmate is supportive and is doing most of the shopping at present.\nHis employer has been understanding about the time off.\nAnxious about the possibility of surgery, which was mentioned on the ward round.\nHis parents live an hour away and visited yesterday; his mother plans to stay with him for the first week at home.\nNon-smoker. Drinks alcohol rarely.\nPlays five-a-side football on Tuesdays and belongs to a local cycling club.\nAsked whether the ward wifi is strong enough for video calls with his work team.\n\nDiscussed:\nThere is no evidence for a restrictive exclusion diet in an acute flare, and food avoidance is worsening the deficit. Explained this and agreed a gradual reintroduction of dairy and refined carbohydrate.\nHe was relieved, saying he had been frightened that eating would make the colitis worse.\nHe accepted a leaflet on eating during a flare and asked for a list of foods to try first.\n\nPlan:\nAssess formally for malnutrition; he meets the criteria on weight loss and low intake.\nRefeeding risk high (low potassium before feeding); thiamine and vitamin B compound before supplements, correct potassium and magnesium, and check potassium, phosphate and magnesium daily for the first three days.\nTwo oral nutritional supplements daily, sipped slowly, starting today.\nSmall, frequent, energy-dense and low-residue meals while symptoms are acute; low-residue in the short term only, relaxed as symptoms settle.\nRecord food and fluid intake on a chart.\nIf intake remains under half of requirements by day six, discuss enteral support with the team; parenteral nutrition only if the gut cannot be used.\nDietetic review twice weekly while an inpatient.\n\nOther information:\nThe inflammatory bowel disease nurse specialist has seen him on the ward and will follow him up after discharge.\nHe prefers written information and is happy to be contacted by email.\nHe has a smartphone and is willing to keep a photo food diary.\nHe is keen to return to running once he is well.\nHis sister is getting married in December and he hopes to be well enough to attend.\nHe asked whether he could have a fan by his bed because the ward is warm.\n",
