      "caseNotes": "\nPatient:\nMrs Sandra Whitfield, 52, female. School administrator.\nSeen in the GP practice, 24 August 2026.\nAttended after an NHS Health Check at the practice in July.\n\nDiagnosis:\nMetabolic syndrome — central obesity, impaired fasting glucose, raised triglycerides, low HDL and raised blood pressure.\nPast history: laparoscopic cholecystectomy for gallstones in 2015; carpal tunnel release in 2018. No ongoing problems.\nTwo pregnancies, both uncomplicated, with no gestational diabetes.\n\nAnthropometry:\nWeight 96 kg. Height 1.63 m. Body mass index 36.1.\nWeighed in light clothing on the practice scales.\nWaist 108 cm.\nMeasured midway between the lowest rib and the top of the hip.\nWeight has risen by approximately 14 kg over six years, with most of the gain since the menopause.\nWeight was 82 kg six years ago.\nLost 6 kg with a commercial slimming club in 2022 but regained it over the following year.\n\nBiochemistry:\nFasting glucose 6.4 mmol/L on two occasions.\nBoth samples taken at the practice after an overnight fast.\nHbA1c 44 mmol/mol.\nTriglycerides 2.8 mmol/L. HDL cholesterol 0.9 mmol/L. Total cholesterol 5.6 mmol/L.\nBlood pressure 148/92, confirmed on home readings.\nHome readings taken twice daily for a week on a validated monitor.\nLiver enzymes mildly raised; ultrasound shows hepatic steatosis.\nThyroid function normal.\nRenal function normal. Urine albumin-creatinine ratio normal.\n\nIntake history:\nTakeaway meals three evenings a week.\nUsually pizza, fish and chips or a Chinese meal, on Fridays and at weekends.\nTwo sugary soft drinks and two sweetened coffees daily.\nEach coffee has two sugars and whole milk.\nGrazes through the evening in front of the television — crisps, chocolate, cheese and crackers.\nThis usually starts after the evening meal and goes on until about 11 pm.\nBreakfast is usually skipped; lunch is a sandwich meal deal at her desk.\nShe says she is not hungry before work and leaves home early.\nAlcohol approximately fourteen units weekly, mostly at weekends.\nDrinks about a litre of water a day at work.\nEnjoys cooking curries and roast dinners for the family at weekends.\nUsually wine with friends on Saturday evenings and a gin and tonic on Sundays.\n\nActivity:\nSedentary role. Drives to work. No planned exercise.\nWorks full-time, Monday to Friday, mostly at a computer. The school is about two miles from home.\nKnee pain on stairs limits her, and she is reluctant to attend a gym.\nThe GP assessed the knee last year as mild osteoarthritis; physiotherapy exercises helped.\nHer husband walks most evenings and has offered to go with her.\nShe used to swim at school and says she might be willing to try a women-only swimming session.\n\nMedication:\nNone currently. She has declined statin therapy twice and wishes to try lifestyle change first.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nSocial:\nLives with her husband and adult son. She does all of the cooking.\nHer husband is supportive and willing to change what the family eats.\nHer son works shifts and often eats separately.\nHer husband has asked whether he can attend the first session with her.\nHer mother developed type 2 diabetes at 55 and this worries her.\nHer daughter lives nearby with a young family.\nNon-smoker; stopped in her twenties.\nRings bells with the church group on Wednesday evenings.\nCervical and breast screening up to date.\nHer mother is now 79 and manages her diabetes with tablets.\n\nMotivation:\nKeen and specific — she wants to avoid medication and to walk her daughter's dog without breathlessness.\nShe understands that her results raise her risk of diabetes and wants to reduce it.\n\nPlan:\nRefer to the structured twelve-week weight management programme starting 21 September.\nDietary modification with attention to evening grazing and to sugary drinks, which alone account for a substantial daily excess.\nPortion control using the plate model.\nIncrease activity gradually, starting with walking, allowing for the knee.\nBehaviour change support and self-monitoring.\nAim for 5 to 10 per cent weight loss over six months.\nMonitor weight, waist, blood pressure and fasting glucose. GP review in three months.\nBlood pressure 148/92 on home readings is hypertension (NICE NG136): GP to calculate her QRISK and discuss treatment, not lifestyle alone.\nHbA1c 44 mmol/mol is non-diabetic hyperglycaemia: offer referral to the NHS Diabetes Prevention Programme.\nFatty liver with raised enzymes: GP to check a FIB-4 score for liver fibrosis (NICE NG49).\n\nOther information:\nCan attend sessions on weekday evenings or Saturday mornings.\nShe asked whether the programme venue has a car park and whether any sessions are online.\nPrefers contact by text message.\nShe has a smartphone and would use an app for food records.\nAlready wears a step-counting watch, which shows about 4000 steps on a work day.\nShe is happy for the programme team to involve her husband if helpful.\n",
